KEYWORDS: surface-enhanced Raman scattering, nanochannel membrane, reactive cavities, exhaled volatile organic compounds, machine learning
L ung cancer is the most prevalent form of cancer and the leading cause of cancer-related mortality worldwide.1 The upregulation of numerous proteins is an important characteristic of lung cancer that makes its prognosis and diagnosis difficult.2 During the pathological processes of lung cancer, the relative concentrations of exhaled volatile organic compounds (VOCs) differ significantly from those produced during normal physiological processes.1,3 Biomarkers for lung cancer have been identified in 42 types of VOCs.4 Studies using gas chromatography/mass spectrometry analysis have shown that the levels of several VOCs in the breath of healthy humans range from 1 to $20\ \mathrm{ppb}$ and increase to 10 to 100 ppb in lung cancer patients.5 In general, these biomarkers can be divided into seven categories: alcohols, hydrocarbons, ketones, aldehydes, nitriles, esters, and aromatic compounds.4 Breath testing is a noninvasive and rapid method for identifying specific VOCs in exhaled breath in order to diagnose medical conditions. Unfortunately, the concentrations of diseaserelated biomarkers are low, and the compositions of exhaled breath are quite complex.6 The development of a platform for the rapid, noninvasive, sensitive, and selective detection of exhaled breath is crucial for the early diagnosis of lung cancer.
Surface-enhanced Raman scattering (SERS) has demonstrated excellent single-molecule detection sensitivity.7−9 Nevertheless, utilizing SERS technology to detect trace gaseous molecules remains difficult. Gaseous molecules have a lower number density and higher kinetic energy than solid or liquid samples, which hinders their capture on SERS hotspots. Due to the possibility of a smaller Raman scattering cross section, they may also necessitate more sensitive SERS probes.10−12 Currently, there are two primary solutions for achieving SERS application in the detection of gaseous molecules: (i) develop a simple and low-cost method to construct substrates with highly ordered plasmon nanostructure (i.e., hotspots) to ensure the high sensitivity and reproducibility of SERS signal of Raman weak strength molecules;13,14 (ii) explore an efficient method to bring gaseous molecules to the surface of plasmon nanostructures and ensure uniform absorption of the gaseous molecules.15 Nevertheless, due to trace concentrations, high mobility, and complex matrix interference in real samples, the majority of SERS-active substrates face obstacles, such as poor affinity between the plasmon surface and analytes, undifferentiated target concentration/enrichment, interfering molecules, etc.
Due to their inherent microporous structure and abundance of functional groups, metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) are commonly used for gas accumulation.16−18 Particularly, the modifiable pore sizes of MOFs enable a molecular sieving effect, whereby size-matched analytes can be selectively adsorbed and accommodated within the structure of MOFs.19,20
Moreover, covering substrate surfaces with MOFs is promising for the surface enrichment of target gas molecules. When coated on a solid substrate surface, a large number of interfacial nanocavities would form between the substrate and MOFs,21 which provides confined chambers for gas molecule accumulation. Recently, such a nanoscale interfacial cavity has been utilized as an effective gas concentrator.22,23 The MOF layer can also serve as a protective coating for the NPs, thereby preventing aggregation and boosting their chemical stability.24 In this study, a SERS sensing platform for gaseous aldehydes was developed based on the novel double-open-ended tube structure of a $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanochannel membrane $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM). A uniform SERS substrate was created by asymmetric coating one side of the nanochannel with gold nanoparticles $\left(\mathrm{Au}\mathrm{NPs}\right)$ using sputtering (Scheme 1A). Au was grafted with 4- aminothiophene (4-ATP) in order to construct a targetresponsive scaffold. As a gas-trapping layer, zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 (ZIF-8) was subsequently applied to the surface. When the gas passes through the nanochannels, the target molecules can be effectively trapped in the porous ZIF-8 (Scheme 1B), thereby slowing their diffusion rate and concentrating them at the interfaces of 4-ATP-pregrafted Au and ZIF-8. With the assistance of the Schiff base reaction with $\scriptstyle4-\mathrm{ATP}^{25}$ and the Raman enhancement effect of $\mathsf{i u N P s}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM, a robust Raman signal was generated. In this system, gas molecules can flow slowly through the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM. This property is conducive to the diffusion of gaseous molecules and consequently provides sufficient modifications to capture the target analyte. Principal component analysis (PCA) and machine learning (ML) were used to differentiate the types of gaseous aldehydes based on SERS spectra by using an appropriate spectral preprocessing procedure. In this study, SERS NM provided a sensing substrate with high sensitivity and specificity for the detection of gaseous aldehydes. Its noninvasive, straightforward, and efficient qualities make it a promising alternative for early lung cancer screening.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Preparation of the $\bar{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ NM. The $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM was produced through a two-step process. Initially, the cleaned Ti foils ( $\cdot15\mathrm{mm}\times15$ $\mathrm{mm}\times0.1\ \mathrm{mm}$ ) were anodized in an ethylene glycol (EG) electrolyte containing 0.1 M $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ (120 V, $20~\mathrm{min}$ ).26,27 The formed samples were then immersed in $30\%$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ until the Ti substrate separated from the membrane in the second step. The membrane was annealed at $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in air for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ at a heating rate of $3~{^\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min}$ .
Preparation and Functionalization of ${\sf A u}/{\sf T i O}_{2}$ NM with 4- ATP. Au NPs were asymmetrically coated onto the base entrance side of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM using a sputtering technique (EM SCD 500, Leica). 4-ATP molecules were attached to the as-formed ${\mathrm{Au/TiO}}_{2}$ NM via covalent bonding (Au−S bonds). The ${\mathrm{Au/TiO}}_{2}$ NM was immersed in a $0.1\ \mathrm{mM}$ 4-ATP ethanol solution at $4~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The sample was then rinsed three times with ethanol to eliminate nonspecifically adsorbed 4-ATP molecules before being dried under nitrogen to produce $4–\mathrm{ATP}/\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM.
Growth of ZIF-8 on 4-ATP-Grafted ${\sf A u}/{\sf T i O}_{2}$ NM. In order to form ZIF-8 on the 4-ATP-grafted $\mathrm{Au/TiO_{2}N M},$ the $4{\cdot}\mathrm{ATP}/\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM was initially immersed in a $15~\mathrm{mL}$ glass container. Separate $2~\mathrm{mL}$ methanolic solutions of $\mathrm{Zn}\big(\mathrm{NO}_{3}\big)_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(25~\mathrm{~mM})$ and 2- methylimidazole (2-Melm, $50~\mathrm{mM}$ ) were prepared and mixed for 5 s. The mixture was placed in the glass container and reacted for 40 min at room temperature. The $\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8/4{-}\mathrm{ATP}/\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM that resulted was washed three times with methanol. The preceding steps were repeated until the desired ZIF-8 thickness was achieved.
Gas Adsorption. Experiments on $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM-based gas adsorption were conducted in an H-type box containing a sample chamber and a reaction chamber (Figure S1). The asymmetric ZIF-8/4-ATP/Au/ $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM was mounted between two boxes. The entrance at the tip faced the sample chamber, and the entrance at the base faced the reaction chamber. By affixing a hot plate to the bottom of the sample chamber, we could produce a gas environment with different aldehyde concentrations could be produced. The gaseous aldehydes reached the reaction zone at the base through the membrane’s tip entrance. The asymmetric substrate was removed for the SERS measurement after $\ensuremath{5}\ensuremath{\mathrm{~min~}}$ .
ML Models. Python (ver. 3.8) was utilized for the ML model programming. Raman measurements of seven aldehydes (3-phenylpropionaldehyde, 4-nitrobenzaldehyde, benzaldehyde, formaldehyde, salicylaldehyde, glutaric dialdehyde, and 4-ethylbenzaldehyde) and interfering gases (hydrocarbons, alcohols, ketones, esters, nitriles, aromatic compounds, organic acid, and $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ ) were performed using Raman microscopy. Each Raman spectrum was recorded with 571 data points between 800 and $1800\mathrm{^{-}c m^{-1}}$ . For each gas, 638 Raman spectra were collected. Eighty percent of the SERS spectra were used to model the spectral set, while the remaining $20\%$ were used for testing. All raw measured spectra were subjected to a preprocessing procedure, which involved removing the background and normalizing the data. Due to the high dimensionality of the preprocessed spectra, the four methods of k-nearest neighbor (KNN), random forest classifier, support vector machine (SVM), and decision tree use PCA to extract 20 principal feature values for dimensionality reduction.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Fabrication and Characterization of the ZIF-8/Au/ $\bar{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ NM. Scheme 1A shows the fabrication of a $\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8/\mathrm{Au}/$ $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM SERS substrate for aldehyde gas detection. Electrochemical anodization was utilized to grow arrays of vertically aligned $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanochannels on a Ti foil. The freestanding $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM was then created by detaching the nanochannel arrays from the Ti foil (details are available in the Experimental Section). As shown by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images, the well-aligned $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM is composed of a large number of nanotubes that are uniformly distributed (Figure S2). The formed $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM consists of nanochannels with two open entrances (Figures 1A and S3). The smooth tube walls of the nanochannels have an average diameter of $150\pm20\ \mathrm{nm}$ at the base entrance side (Figure 1A) and $40\pm10~\mathrm{{nm}}$ at the tip entrance side (Figure S3). This research produced a membrane with a thickness of ${\sim}37.4\ \mu\mathrm{m}$ (Figure S4A). Figure S4 depicts a cross-sectional view of the membrane, which reveals additional structural details. It is well known that a high yield of “hotspots” can enhance the Raman signal. Magnetron sputtering was used to asymmetrically modify Au NPs on the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM for this purpose. The size of Au NPs at the entrance of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM grows with increasing sputtering times (Figure S5). In this study, 4-ATP was used as a model Raman probe to evaluate the SERS properties of various materials. There are two distinct reasons for using 4- ATP as a Raman probe. Due to the chemical interaction between the thiol groups of 4-ATP and the Au surface, 4-ATP molecules can self-assemble spontaneously into a monolayer (SAM) on the Au surface. The chemisorption of 4-ATP on the Au surface ensures that any changes in the vibrational spectrum are the result of the interaction between 4-ATP-Au and aldehydes. Second, the aromatic moieties of the surfacegrafted 4-ATP are able to generate potent SERS characteristics that can vary systematically with their chemical environment. This is crucial for monitoring the molecular processes occurring on the Au surface during gas injection. The SERS signals were observed to increase with the Au sputtering duration, with the strongest SERS activity observed at $20~\mathrm{min}$ (Figure S6). At this sputtering time, not only did the number and size of the Au NPs growing on the base entrance surface of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM decrease but so did the distance between adjacent Au NPs (Figure S5E). SERS spectra are typically dominated by molecules located within the range of SERS hotspots $\left(1-5~\mathrm{nm}\right)$ .28,29 It should also be noted that for the obtained high-performance SERS substrates, Au NPs exhibit an asymmetrical distribution mainly located on the surface of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM (Figure 1B) and the base entrance portion of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM (Figure 1C).
The sample was submerged in a mixture of methanolic $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}\bar{6}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and 2-Melm, and the ${\bf A u}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM was coated with ZIF-8 (the resulting material was designated as the $\mathrm{ZIF{-}8/A u/T i O_{2}\ N M}$ ). The thickness of the ZIF-8 film above the ${\bf A u}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM can be modified by increasing the growth times (Figure S7). The thickness and coverage of ZIF-8 on ${\mathrm{Au/TiO}}_{2}$ NM may influence the number of preconcentrated gas molecules near the plasma nanoparticle surface. To evaluate the SERS response of 4-ATP, we placed ZIF-8/Au/ $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM with different ZIF-8 growth times in a gaseous environment of 4-ATP. Figures S8 and S9 depict the characteristic peaks of 4-ATP at $1078~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ (C−S stretching vibration) and $1589~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ (benzene ring $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C}}={\mathrm{C}}$ stretching vibration) on Z $\mathrm{IF}{-}8/\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM-based SERS substrates prepared by varying ZIF-8 decoration times. The Raman signals increased with the ZIF- $\mathbf{8^{\prime}s}$ thickness, but this trend slowed after $100~\mathrm{{min}}$ . Based on the preceding results, $100~\mathrm{{min}}$ was chosen as the ZIF-8 growth time for the following investigation.
Figures 1D,E and S10 display SEM images of the final sample. Nanochannel base entrances are uniformly covered with numerous ZIF-8 nanocrystals. On the other hand, there are only a few ZIF-8 nanocrystals on the tip entrance side of ${\bf A u}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM, which has a well-opened tip entrance (Figure
1F). Using transmission electron microscopy (TEM), the morphology of the Z $\mathrm{IF}{-}8/\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM was also characterized. As demonstrated in Figure 1G, the sample maintains the typical nanochannel structure, and Au NPs and ZIF-8 were observed on one side of the nanochannels (Figure 1H). In Figure 1I, the high-resolution TEM (HR-TEM) image shows the characteristic lattice spacings for the (111) plane of Au NPs and the (101) plane of anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . In addition, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) based on the appearance of $\mathbf{N}$ 1s and $\mathtt{Z n\ 2p}$ signals confirmed the successful growth of ZIF-8 (Figures S11 and S12). Furthermore, energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) mapping images, X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns, and Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectra demonstrated the successful modification of ZIF-8 on the ${\bf A u}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM. Zn and N elements are uniformly distributed on the channel wall in EDS mapping images (Figure S13). The signals appearing at 7.5, 10.6, 13.0, and $18.2^{\circ}$ in the XRD patterns correspond to (011), (002), (112), and (222) ZIF-8 facets, respectively (Figure S14A). These diffraction peaks correspond to the pure ZIF-8 powder (Figure S14B). The diffraction band at $44.6^{\circ}$ corresponds to singlecrystalline Au NPs with predominantly (200) faces. Figure S15 shows that the FTIR bands at ${\sim}995$ , 1144, 1307, and 1577 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ are consistent with the absorption bands measured for the ZIF-8 powder. In addition, digital images (Figure S16), $\zeta-$ potentials (Figure S17), and Raman spectra (Figure S18) of the nanochannel membrane at each modification stage verify the successful synthesis of ZIF-8 at the membrane’s base entrance. Using atomic force microscopy (AFM, Figures $1\mathrm{J-L}$ and S19), the topography of the base entrance side was further examined. Pure $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM exhibited a uniform pore distribution and a smooth nanochannel entrance (Figures 1J and S19A). Numerous small particles were observed at the entrance after the Au sputtering process (Figures 1K and S19B), and the nanochannel entrance became visibly rougher after ZIF-8 assembly (Figures 1L and S19C).
Characterization of Asymmetric NM. To visualize the asymmetric spatial arrangement of ZIF-8 on one side of the nanochannels, fluorescence images of the samples were captured following incubation of Z $\mathrm{IF}{-}8/\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM in an aqueous solution containing rhodamine B (RhB, $0.56\ \mathrm{nm}\ \times$ $1.18\ \mathrm{nm}\times1.59\ \mathrm{nm},$ a typical red fluorescent probe). Using a mixture of phosphoric acid $\mathrm{(40~mM)}$ in $N,N$ -dimethylformamide as the etching agent, the pore of the ZIF-8 can be tailored through an acid etching technique.30 RhB molecules could fit within the mesopores formed mesopores of ZIF-8. As shown in Figure 1M, under an excitation wavelength of $530{-}550\ \mathrm{nm},$ red fluorescence was only observed at the base entrance of the nanochannels membrane, confirming the successful modification of ZIF-8 only at the base entrance. The fluorescence results are more visible, consistent with the SEM image in Figure 1E.
SERS Performance. To preserve the regular SERS structure of the Au/ $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM, ZIF-8 was fabricated by using a relatively gentle method. Figure S20 depicts the visible excitation wavelength dependence phenomena obtained from SERS spectra of 4-ATP under 532, 638, and $785~\mathrm{\nm}$ excitations. Under the $638\ \mathrm{nm}$ laser excitation, the ZIF-8/ ${\bf A u}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM exhibits a higher Raman signal intensity at the SAM level, indicating that a $638~\mathrm{nm}$ laser can be considered the optimal excitation light for this study. On the basis of the peak intensity of 4-ATP at $1078~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ on a silicon wafer and Z $\mathrm{IF}{-}8/\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM (Figure S21), the enhancement factors (EFs) of ZIF $\dot{\iota}_{-}8/\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM were estimated to be $2.9\times10^{6}$ . The extraordinary EFs were determined to be primarily attributable to the powerful electromagnetic enhancement of Au NPs and the molecular enrichment of ZIF-8. In the evaluation of a Raman platform for sensing applications, reproducibility, uniformity, and stability are also crucial factors. The SERS spectra of $0.1~\mathrm{\mM}$ 4-ATP obtained from 10 randomly selected ZIF- $8/\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM substrates were measured (Figure 2A). Figure 2B illustrates that the relative standard deviation (RSD) of the SERS signals collected from all samples is 4.58 and $5.22\%$ for the Raman peaks at 1078 and $1589~\mathrm{{\bar{cm}}^{-1}}$ , respectively. The low RSD values demonstrated the satisfactory reproducibility of the SERS substrate’s satisfactory reproducibility. The uniformity of the signal was investigated further by acquiring SERS spectra at 49 random locations on a single sample. As shown in Figure 2C, the collected Raman mapping exhibited the uniform pixel intensity of the 4-ATP peak at $\overline{{1078}}\mathrm{~cm}^{-1}$ , with an RSD of $3.70\%$ , indicating that the homogeneous morphology of the proposed SERS substrate provided a highly repeatable spectral response (Figure S22). Moreover, the Raman intensity of $0.1\ \mathrm{mM}\ 4-$ ATP recorded from the aged SERS substrate degraded only slightly, which is comparable to the Raman intensity of the fresh substrate, indicating the ZIF-8 $\mathrm{\backslash/Au/TiO}_{2}$ NM’s durability (Figure 2D).
Characterization of the Interfacial Cavities between ${\sf A u}/{\sf T i O}_{2}$ NM and ZIF-8. In Figure 3A, the ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption and desorption isotherms reveal that the Brunauer−Emmett−
Teller (BET) surface area of $\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8/\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM was $176.7712\ {\mathrm m}^{2}\ {\mathrm g}^{-1}.$ , which is significantly greater than that of the ${\mathrm{Au/TiO}}_{2}$ NM ( $19.2936\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . The significant increase in the surface area is attributable to the porous ZIF-8 coating on the ${\bf A u}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM surface. As shown in Figure 3B, it is hypothesized that the covering of ZIF-8 on the $\mathbf{Au}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\ \mathbf{NM}$ surface will generate molecular-scale interfacial cavities between Au and ZIF-8. Under ambient conditions, the interspace between the SERS-active ${\mathrm{Au/TiO}}_{2}$ NM and the aldehyde gas-absorbent ZIF-8 layer plays a significant role in driving a Schiff base reaction in this system. XPS analyses of Au prior to ZIF-8 formation revealed characteristic Au 0 bands at 82.9 and $86.6\mathrm{~eV}$ (Figure 3C), whereas the XPS bands at 161.0 and $168.0\ \mathrm{~eV}$ (Figure 3D) correspond to the $\mathrm{~\normalfont~S~}2\mathrm{p}$ orbital assigned to the $_\mathrm{Au-S-R}$ chemical moiety. After the coverage of ZIF-8 on $4{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{ATP/Au/TiO}_{2}$ NM, the binding energies of the aforementioned XPS signals keep their initial positions, with $<0.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ change in binding energy. In addition, the qualitative SERS evaluation of $4{\cdot}\mathrm{ATP}/\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM revealed characteristic $C{-}S$ stretching and phenyl stretching motions of 4-ATP at 1078 and $1589~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively (Figure S23), which remain largely unchanged upon ZIF-8 formation (<1 cm−1 shift). The consistency of XPS and SERS characteristics of the 4 $\mathrm{\Delta\mathrm{4-ATP/Au/TiO_{2}\Delta N}}$ M, even after ZIF-8 growth, suggests that there is no direct interaction between ZIF-8 and the grafted 4- ATP monolayer or the underlying Au. Thus, our XPS and SERS analyses of the ZIF-8/4-ATP/Au/ $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM indicate that the overgrowth of the coordination network occurs on top of the monolayer of 4-ATP, as opposed to being directly attached to the Au surfaces. By electron microscopy, the formed molecular-scale interfacial cavities (between Au and ZIF-8) are difficult to characterize. Due to the obvious decrease in free energy at the geometry-specific interface caused by gas sorption, it has been reported that these gaseous molecules could be physically concentrated in the completely embedded cavities.31,32 When gas molecules flow through a membrane composed of nanochannels, the gas diffusion rate is slowed, which increases the likelihood of gas accumulation in the cavities of ZIF-8. Consequently, it is anticipated that more molecules will be captured at the ${\bf A u}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM surface. Interfering molecules are distinct (no $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C}}={\mathrm{O}}$ bonds in their structure) because they cannot be entrapped by the scaffolding and therefore cannot generate interfering signals (Figure 3E). As the adsorption capacity of MOFs is also effective for other molecules, however, for $\operatorname{Au}\ @\operatorname{ZIF}-8$ core−shell nanoparticles, some nonreactive interfering molecules can be fixed in the hotspots area, which reduces the accuracy and reliability of SERS signals (Figure 3F).
Detection of Gaseous Aldehydes on $\mathbf{Z}|\mathsf{F}{-}8/\mathsf{A u}/\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ NM. In exhaled breath, benzaldehyde is one of the classic VOC biomarkers for lung cancer. Due to the small Raman cross section of benzaldehyde molecules with polar bonds $\scriptstyle\left({\mathrm{C}}=0\right)$ ), it is difficult to detect benzaldehyde by SERS with high sensitivity. In our system, the Raman-active 4-ATP molecules gain access to benzaldehyde via the Schiff base reaction (Figures 4A and S24). $\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ group of 4-ATP reacts with the −CHO group of gaseous aldehydes. The interfacial nanocavities between Au NPs and MOFs provided more opportunities for target gas molecules to interact with the sensor surface. The slight shift in $1589{-}1571~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is due to the effective $\pi$ electron conjugation between the benzene ring of benzaldehyde and the amine group on the SERS substrate. Figure 4B attributes a peak that appeared at $1621~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ to the mode of stretching of the $\mathrm{C}{=}\mathrm{N}$ .11,33 The Schiff base reaction was initiated immediately and completed within 5 min (Figure S25).
Figure 4C presents the SERS spectra recorded on a ZIF-8/ ${\bf A u}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM in response to various concentrations of benzaldehyde. Figure 4D displays a linear relationship $\left(R^{2}=\right.$ 0.998) between the intensity ratio of $I_{1621}/I_{1078}$ and the benzaldehyde concentration in the range of $0.5\ \mathrm{ppb}$ to 100 ppm. The ZI $\mathsf{F}{-}8/\mathsf{A u}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM’s limit of detection (LOD) for benzaldehyde was $0.19\mathrm{ppb}$ (defined as mean of blank $+3\sigma,$ , $\sigma=$ standard deviation of blank).34 Figure S26 demonstrates that $\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8/\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\mathrm{NM}$ has a lower LOD and a wider linear range for the detection of benzaldehyde compared to ${\mathrm{Au/TiO}}_{2}$ NM. The high sensitivity can be attributed to (i) the formation of molecular-scale cavities at the interface of Au NPs and ZIF-8 and (ii) the large specific surface area and intrinsic porosity of ZIF-8, which facilitate the enrichment of gas molecules in the hotspots region of SERS. The fluorescence probe ( $\scriptstyle{o-{\frac{}{}}}$ phthalaldehyde) adsorption experiment was performed to gain a direct understanding of the adsorption capability of $\mathrm{Au}/$ $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM and $\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8/\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM on gaseous molecules (Figure S27). The as-formed ZIF- $8/\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM exhibited a more uniform and intense blue fluorescence than the ${\mathrm{Au/TiO}}_{2}$ NM, indicating that ZIF-8 can significantly enhance target adsorption. On Ti foils, a planar $\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8/\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ SERS substrate was prepared to demonstrate the structural advantage of nanochannels in gas trapping. Figure S28 shows the SERS spectra of $10\ \mathrm{ppb}$ benzaldehyde acquired on the nanochannel membrane and the planar sheet. The Raman intensity ratio $\left(I_{1621}/I_{1078}\right)$ of benzaldehyde on the nanochannel sample is much higher than that of the planar substrate, indicating that the nanochannels have a greater number of target molecule adsorption sites. Moreover, the Raman intensity ratio $(I_{1621}/$ $I_{1078})$ of benzaldehyde on the ZIF- ${\cdot8/\mathrm{Au}}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM sample $\left(86.3\%\right)$ was much higher than that of the ZIF-8 tightly covered ${\bf A u}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM (denoted as Z $\mathrm{\Delta^{\prime}I F{-}}8@\mathrm{Au/TiO}_{2}$ NM) substrate $\left(69.6\%\right)$ , which confirmed that the gap between the ZIF-8 and Au NPs is important for capturing and accumulating gas molecules (Figure S29). To verify the accuracy of the sensing method, the concentrations of the benzaldehyde samples were also verified by gas chromatography−mass spectrometry (GC-MS). As shown in Figure S30, the detection results achieved by GC-MS and SERS techniques were 10 and $9.9\ \mathrm{ppm}$ , respectively, thus confirming the reliability of the asproposed SERS sensing platform in benzaldehyde detection. Our sensor system is more sensitive than other benzaldehyde detection methods reported in recent years, such as fluorescence, gas sensing analyzers, and GC (Table S1). In addition, compared to the recently reported various SERS materials for the detection of gaseous aldehydes, the SERS substrate prepared in this study exhibited apparent LOD value advantages (Table S2).
Size Sieving, Versatility, and Selectivity. The ability of the guest molecule to diffuse into the MOFs is dependent not only on the guest, aperture, and pore sizes (size selection property) but also on the framework’s flexibility. The nonstatic pore aperture of ZIF-8 (simulated diameter: $3.{\dot{4}}{\mathring{\mathrm{A}}}{\dot{}}$ ) can expand to $7.6\mathrm{~\AA~}$ or larger $(11.6\ \mathring\mathrm{A})$ , allowing the adsorption of gasphase small VOCs biomarkers.19,35 On the $\mathsf{Z I F{-}}8/\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2},$ NM excludes molecules larger than the pore aperture (Figure 5A). For example, salicylaldehyde $\hat{(}\sim5.9\hat{4}\hat{\mathrm{~\normalfont~A)~}}$ , glutaric dialdehyde $(\sim6.4$ Å), and benzaldehyde $({\sim}5.9\mathrm{~\normalfont~\bar{A}~})$ can penetrate ZI $\mathrm{F}{-}8/\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM. Despite the fact that the kinetic diameter of 2-naphthaldehyde $({\sim}7.8\mathrm{\\AA})$ is marginally larger than the pore size of ZIF-8 $(7.6\mathrm{\AA})$ , the MOF structure only permits a small amount of 2-naphthaldehyde to pass through the ZIF-8 layer.20 As the kinetic diameter of pyrene-1- carboxaldehyde $(\sim12.1$ Å) is significantly larger than the aperture of ZIF-8, the molecule cannot diffuse into the pores of ZIF-8. Consequently, the ZI $\vec{\cdot}-8/\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM exhibited a robust Raman response to salicylaldehyde, glutaric dialdehyde, and benzaldehyde but a weak response to 2-naphthaldehyde (Figure 5B). The detection of pyrene-1-carboxaldehyde revealed no Raman response. The MOF shell layer plays a pre-exclusion role in the complex VOCs system for the selective detection of gas targets. To differentiate between different aldehyde species, fingerprint information was identified by Raman spectra, followed by PCA-based statistical analysis of the Raman peaks. Seven distinct aldehyde clusters were obtained, each representing a distinct analyte. Eight distinct Raman bands were observed in the Raman spectra (Figure S31). As depicted in Figure 5C, the two-dimensional PCA score plot displays the points in each cluster for VOC detections measured by ZIF- $3/\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM as proposed. The distinct separation between the seven clusters of aldehydes demonstrates the excellent reproducibility of the SERS sensor results. Even aldehydes with high chemical similarity are accurately classified, indicating that the SERS sensor has a high capacity for discrimination. Benefiting from the Schiff base reaction between the $\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ group on the SERS substrate and the −CHO group, this sensing system demonstrated a high degree of anti-interference, as demonstrated by the examination of other potential interference biomarkers for lung cancer, including alcohols (ethyl alcohol), hydrocarbons $n$ -hexane), ketones (acetone), organic acid (acetic acid), esters (ethyl acetate), aromatic compounds (methylbenzene), nitriles (acetonitrile), and $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ (Figure S32 and inset of Figure 5D). Figure 5D depicts the Raman intensity ratio of $I_{1621}/I_{1078}$ for various analytes. In stark contrast, only benzaldehyde exhibited a high $I_{1621}/I_{1078}$ intensity ratio. Considering that human breath contains more than $80\%$ relative humidity, the effect of humidity on sensor performance was also investigated (Figure S33). Due to the low Raman scattering ability of water molecules, the measurement of relative humidity has no effect on the sensor’s performance.
ML-Assisted Aldehydes Gas Identification. In exhaled gases, various types of biomarkers frequently coexist as a mixture. In order to achieve a precise and dependable sensing result for aldehyde biomarkers in complex exhaled breath, an ML algorithm was developed to eliminate interference from other components. Based on the ZIF-8/4-ATP/Au/ $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM, the SERS spectra of seven types of gaseous aldehydes were collected and analyzed using six supervised ML models (Figure 6A), including convolutional neural networks (CNN), KNN, deep neural networks (DNN), random forest classifier, SVM, and decision tree. Eighty percent of the spectra of various gaseous aldehydes were used to model spectral sets, whereas $20\%$ were used for testing. Confusion matrices were utilized to quantitatively evaluate the performance of the six ML models. The confusion matrices circumvent the accuracy limitation, which can be misleading when there is a significant disparity between two categories. The confusion matrices provide a table for visualizing the results from the six models, thereby revealing the actual performance of each model, which is concealed by the accuracy metrics. Figure $6\mathrm{B-G}$ presents the normalized confusion matrices of the six models. Among the seven aldehydes and eight interfering specimens, all models showed good prediction with a classification accuracy of above $96\%$ . These high localization accuracies for aldehydes demonstrate the benefits of SERS-based odor sensing.
## CONCLUSIONS
In conclusion, we developed an extremely sensitive SERS substrate for the selective detection of aldehyde biomarkers in the exhaled breath of lung cancer patients. The proposed ZIF$8/\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NM has an enhanced Raman signal when detecting gaseous analytes due to the uniform alignment of Au NPs. When gas molecules flow through two-end open nanochannels, a higher gas capture efficiency is achieved. Particularly, the interfacial nanocavities between Au NPs and MOFs function as gas confinement cavities to circumvent the gas sensing limitations imposed by the difficulty of gas molecule absorption on solid substrates. Using Schiff base reaction between pregrafted Raman-active 4-ATP and the target aldehydes, it demonstrates ppb-level sensitivity. Statistical analysis of Raman spectra in conjunction with PCA and ML was used to model the aldehydes in exhaled breath. This research demonstrates that the sensor holds great promise for biomarker screening assays for lung cancer.
